## Construct candidates from source parts

`Finite_Candidate_Assembly` declares the complete finite language of subsets of a supplied part basis whose combined coverage satisfies the requirements. The constructor chooses an uncovered requirement with the fewest available supporting parts, adds a supporting part, and removes redundant parts after coverage is complete. It returns a candidate exactly when that language is nonempty. Every returned candidate is inclusion minimal, and removing any retained part exposes a requirement that the remaining parts cannot cover. Search order may choose another sufficient candidate; no minimum-cardinality claim is made.

The application consumer uses actual schema observation sites as parts. A requirement carries the complete problem, target application and variable. A site covers it only through a whole accepted matching relation contained in that target's binding. The shared observation-assembly equation connects this coverage to generation of the complete binding. The application equation then connects it to the original instance and material checks and preservation of every mandatory application across the supplied problem family.

The input gate checks the complete schema, all supplied call terms, the original premise enumeration and an exact presentation of the mandatory targets. Mandatory targets include both explicitly required applications and every application of the original separate-source operation. Empty-variable targets still pass those checks. Construction reports input validity, uncovered requirements and the optional plan separately. An empty candidate comparison cannot stand in for a successful construction.

`application_candidate_subjects` presents the plan returned by this constructor. Its declared language includes all sufficient subsets of the supplied site basis. The former separate and joined application operations remain available as preservation controls. The constructor does not claim coverage of every possible program or development approach, and externally proposed proof changes remain externally proposed.

## Search compatible contributions for the required scope

`Finite_Compatible_Unions` retains the full meaning of all functional unions of whole supplied fragments. Its earlier include/exclude equation is preserved. `Finite_Directed_Contributions` separately finds exactly the members with the demanded variable domain. It selects an uncovered variable with few distinct available values, retains only complete fragments compatible with each partial assignment, and rejects unsupported requirements. A completed assignment is returned only when whole fragments support every value together.

The scoped method is proved equal to filtering the original full compatible-union meaning. It serves complete schema application construction through a separate code equation. The complete instance and material checks remain in that equation. The method can still have many results when the problem has many distinct complete bindings; the semantic result set is not truncated by a host work limit.

The execution evidence must retain complete inputs, code and proof identities, successful results, malformed and impossible controls, original-reader regressions, larger source bases and all failed attempts. A runtime timeout is an incomplete measurement, not proof that a candidate does not exist. Native subject equations, actual execution, independent result assessment and conditional host decisions are distinct parts of that evidence.
